pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Explain what OCR can and cannot do and name the flaws of recognised text, which prepared images reduce and which a person must correct"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can explain what OCR can and cannot do and name the flaws of recognised text, which prepared images reduce and which a person must correct, at the understand level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter22.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand  .
pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Read text from an image with Pillow and PyTesseract, choose the language with lang, and tell what happens when a language pack is missing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can read text from an image with Pillow and PyTesseract, choose the language with lang, and tell what happens when a language pack is missing, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter22.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Prepare an image for recognition with cropping, a border, inversion and contrast, and predict what each does to a grid of pixel values"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can prepare an image for recognition with cropping, a border, inversion and contrast, and predict what each does to a grid of pixel values, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter22.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Build and read the command-line call of NAPS2 as a list for subprocess, including its input slice notation, and check the result of the call"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can build and read the command-line call of NAPS2 as a list for subprocess, including its input slice notation, and check the result of the call, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter22.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Analyze which checks on recognised text a program can make for itself and which need a person"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can analyze which checks on recognised text a program can make for itself and which need a person, at the analyze level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter22.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze  .
pg:Q1 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What is the edit distance (the least number of single-letter changes) between 'kitten' and 'sitting'?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q1_O0, pg:Q1_O1, pg:Q1_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q1_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Two changes are not enough: k to s and e to i leave an extra g to add." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Three is the least: change k to s, change e to i, add g." .
pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "2" .
pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "3" .
pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "4" .
pg:Q2 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A program compares recognised text with the known text and the edit distance is 1 for a known text of 4 letters. What is the character error rate?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q2_O0, pg:Q2_O1, pg:Q2_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q2_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "One would mean every letter was wrong; the rate is the distance divided by the length of the known text." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The rate divides by the length of the known text (4), not by 25." .
pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "0.25" .
pg:Q2_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "1.0" .
pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "0.04" .
pg:Q3 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "For the line 'Order 4417-B shipped on 2026-10-13' Tesseract was told tessedit_char_whitelist=0123456789- . It returned '4417-02026-10-13'. What does that show?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q3_O0, pg:Q3_O1, pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q3_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Tesseract gives no such warning; it picks the closest allowed character." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "In this run the B became a 0, so a whitelist forces an answer instead of admitting doubt." .
pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The whitelist made Tesseract say it was unsure about the B" .
pg:Q3_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The B was forced into an allowed character, a 0, and the letters and spaces vanished" .
pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The whitelist only hides characters the engine already found" .
pg:Q4 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "The timeout argument of image_to_string was set so short that Tesseract could not finish. Which exception did pytesseract 0.3.13 raise?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q4_O0, pg:Q4_O1, pg:Q4_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q4_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is not what the wrapper raised in this study." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "TesseractError is raised when Tesseract itself reports a failure, such as a missing language." .
pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "TimeoutError" .
pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "RuntimeError" .
pg:Q4_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "TesseractError" .
pg:Q5 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "lang='ger' was passed to image_to_string for a German line. What happened, and what is the right code?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q5_O0, pg:Q5_O1, pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q5_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Tesseract uses ISO 639-3 codes such as deu, not 639-2 abbreviations." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Tesseract raised an error rather than returning an empty string; and de is not a Tesseract code." .
pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It worked, because ger is short for German" .
pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "TesseractError: there is no ger.traineddata; the code is deu" .
pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It returned an empty string; the code is de" .
pg:Q6 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A line holds the words 'The cat is' then 猫 then 'and the dog is' then 犬 then 'today'. What did lang='eng' alone give, and which setting read it right?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q6_O0, pg:Q6_O1, pg:Q6_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q6_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "English-only data has no Japanese characters, so it cannot give them right." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "In this study both eng+jpn and jpn+eng read the line right and no error was raised." .
pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It gave 'The cat is 4 and the dog is % today'; lang='eng+jpn' read it right" .
pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It gave the whole line right; lang='jpn' broke it" .
pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It raised TesseractError; lang='jpn+eng' is the only order that works" .
pg:Q7 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A PDF has seven pages, spam0 to spam6. Which pages does the NAPS2 input 'spam.pdf[1:6:2]' take?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q7_O0, pg:Q7_O1, pg:Q7_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q7_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The slice starts at index 1, not 2." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The step of 2 skips every other page." .
pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "spam1, spam3 and spam5" .
pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "spam2, spam4 and spam6" .
pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "spam1 to spam6" .
pg:Q8 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "subprocess.run(['python', '-c', 'raise SystemExit(2)'], check=True) is called. What happens?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q8_O0, pg:Q8_O1, pg:Q8_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q8_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Without check=True it would return; check=True turns a failing exit status into an exception." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The program exists, so there is no FileNotFoundError." .
pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It returns a result whose returncode is 2" .
pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It raises CalledProcessError with returncode 2" .
pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It raises FileNotFoundError" .
pg:Q9 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A program opens output.txt three times with open('output.txt', 'a') and writes 'abc\\n' each time. What does the file hold?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q9_O0, pg:Q9_O1, pg:Q9_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q9_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Mode 'w' would leave one line; mode 'a' appends." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Append mode keeps what is there and adds to the end." .
pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'abc\\n'" .
pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'abc\\nabc\\nabc\\n'" .
pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Nothing, because the file is opened again each time" .
pg:Q10 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does re.sub(r'-\\n(\\w)', r'\\1', 'the half-\\nextinguished light') return, and what does it show about joining hyphenated line ends in OCR text?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q10_O0, pg:Q10_O1, pg:Q10_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q10_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The pattern removes the hyphen and the line break." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The replacement has no space." .
pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'the half-extinguished light': the hyphen is kept" .
pg:Q10_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'the halfextinguished light': a real hyphen is removed too, so the joining can damage text" .
pg:Q10_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'the half extinguished light': a space replaces the hyphen" .
pg:Q11 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A line of text cropped tight to its letters was read as an empty string by Tesseract 5.3.4 in its default mode. Which change gave 'Tesseract reads this line' in this study?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q11_O0, pg:Q11_O1, pg:Q11_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q11_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A tilt makes reading worse: five degrees already gave an error rate of 0.58." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Inverting is advice for white text on black, not the cause of an empty result here." .
pg:Q11_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Adding a white border of 10 pixels" .
pg:Q11_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Rotating the image by 12 degrees" .
pg:Q11_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Inverting it to white text on black" .
pg:InstallingTheTools a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Installing the tools"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first part of the chapter, Installing Tesseract and PyTesseract, tells the reader to install the free Tesseract OCR engine on Windows, macOS or Linux, optionally with language packs, and then the PyTesseract package, which also installs Pillow (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract documentation describes the engine as a command line program that is installed with apt on Linux, with brew on macOS and with an installer from UB Mannheim on Windows (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_InstallingTheTools> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InstallingTheTools" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InstallingTheTools .
pg:TheEngine a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "The engine"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says PyTesseract works with the Tesseract OCR engine, a separate program that must be installed first, and that on Windows its folder must be on the PATH so that PyTesseract can reach tesseract.exe (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract command line documentation lists the options --version and --list-langs of that program (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_TheEngine> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TheEngine" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InstallingTheTools .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TheEngine .
pg:EngineVersion a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Engine version"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter never prints the version of Tesseract it used; it says only that the exact results may vary as the language packs are updated (Sweigart, 2025). Wikipedia's article says version 4, released in 2018, added the LSTM-based engine, and version 5 was released in 2021 (Wikipedia, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_EngineVersion> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EngineVersion" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TheEngine .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EngineVersion .
pg:LanguageFolder a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Language folder"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter lists the installed language packs through tess.get_languages() and says the packs are files with a .traineddata extension, such as jpn.traineddata (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract command line documentation says --list-langs lists the languages whose data files are installed, and --tessdata-dir names another folder (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_LanguageFolder> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LanguageFolder" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TheEngine .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LanguageFolder .
pg:PathLookup a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Path lookup"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that on Windows the installation folder must be added to the PATH variable so PyTesseract can access tesseract.exe, and that Chapter 12 covered the PATH (Sweigart, 2025). The pytesseract README says that when the executable is not on the PATH, the variable pytesseract.pytesseract.tesseract_cmd names it (Pytesseract contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_PathLookup> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PathLookup" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TheEngine .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PathLookup .
pg:TheWrapper a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "The wrapper"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter introduces PyTesseract as the Python package that works with the Tesseract engine and says it installs Pillow with it; Appendix A pins pytesseract==0.3.10 and pillow==10.4.0 (Sweigart, 2025). The PyPI page of pytesseract lists 0.3.13, uploaded on 2024-08-16, as the newest release (Python Package Index, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_TheWrapper> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TheWrapper" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InstallingTheTools .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TheWrapper .
pg:PyTesseractRelease a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Py tesseract release"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to install the latest version of PyTesseract by following Appendix A, which pins pytesseract==0.3.10 (Sweigart, 2025). PyPI lists 28 releases of pytesseract, from 0.1 (2014-02-06) to 0.3.13 (2024-08-16), with no 0.3.11 and no 0.3.12 among them (Python Package Index, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_PyTesseractRelease> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PyTesseractRelease" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TheWrapper .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PyTesseractRelease .
pg:FindLoaderBreak a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Find loader break"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix A pins pytesseract==0.3.10 (Sweigart, 2025). The page of what is new in Python 3.14 lists the removal of pkgutil.find_loader, with importlib.util.find_spec as its replacement (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_FindLoaderBreak> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FindLoaderBreak" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TheWrapper .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FindLoaderBreak .
pg:PillowCoupling a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pillow coupling"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter opens the image with Image.open() and passes the Image object to tess.image_to_string(); Appendix B answer 2 says PyTesseract works with the Pillow image library (Sweigart, 2025). The pytesseract README shows image_to_string called with an opened image and with a file name (Pytesseract contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_PillowCoupling> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PillowCoupling" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TheWrapper .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PillowCoupling .
pg:InstallerPlatforms a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Installer platforms"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter gives one installer per platform: the UB Mannheim installer and the PATH on Windows, Homebrew on macOS (brew install tesseract and brew install tesseract-lang) and apt on Linux (sudo apt install tesseract-ocr, or tesseract-ocr-all) (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract installation page lists apt, brew and the UB Mannheim installers (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_InstallerPlatforms> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InstallerPlatforms" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InstallingTheTools .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InstallerPlatforms .
pg:WindowsInstaller a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Windows installer"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to download the installer from the UB Mannheim page, to tick the boxes for additional script and language data, and to add C:\\Program Files\\Tesseract-OCR to the PATH (Sweigart, 2025). The page github.com/UB-Mannheim/tesseract/wiki answered HTTP 403 to the study's single request, so the installer page itself was not read (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_WindowsInstaller> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WindowsInstaller" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InstallerPlatforms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WindowsInstaller .
pg:MacHomebrew a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Mac homebrew"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to install Homebrew from docs.brew.sh, then run brew install tesseract and, for the other languages, brew install tesseract-lang (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract installation page lists brew among the ways to install the engine (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_MacHomebrew> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MacHomebrew" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InstallerPlatforms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MacHomebrew .
pg:LinuxApt a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Linux apt"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says sudo apt install tesseract-ocr installs the engine, sudo apt install tesseract-ocr-all every language, and a three-character code such as fra, deu or jpn in place of all one language (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract installation page lists the apt packages (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_LinuxApt> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LinuxApt" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InstallerPlatforms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LinuxApt .
pg:ReadingAnImage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Reading an image"@en ;
    skos:definition "The second part, OCR Fundamentals, shows the whole job in four lines: import pytesseract as tess and Pillow's Image, open ocr-example.png, and pass the Image to tess.image_to_string(), which returns the text (Sweigart, 2025). It then scans a page of Frankenstein printed in 1831 (Figure 22-1) and finds that the result has mistakes (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ReadingAnImage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReadingAnImage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReadingAnImage .
pg:TheFourLines a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "The four lines"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's four lines import the two modules, open the image, call tess.image_to_string(img) and print the text, which is the first part of the introduction of The Big Book of Small Python Projects and ends with --snip-- (Sweigart, 2025). The pytesseract README documents image_to_string and its keyword arguments (Pytesseract contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_TheFourLines> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TheFourLines" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ReadingAnImage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TheFourLines .
pg:OpenImage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Open image"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to download ocr-example.png from the book's online resources and open it with Image.open() (Sweigart, 2025). The online materials, a zip of 7,759,877 bytes with 91 files, hold ocr-example.png, frankenstein.png, frankenstein_jpn.png and preprocess_ocr.py, and the study fetched the zip and checked the SHA-256 of each (No Starch Press, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_OpenImage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OpenImage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TheFourLines .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OpenImage .
pg:ImageToString a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image to string"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls tess.image_to_string(img) and says Python makes the sophisticated algorithms accessible with four lines of code (Sweigart, 2025). Appendix B answer 3 says the function accepts an Image object and returns a string (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ImageToString> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageToString" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TheFourLines .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageToString .
pg:ReturnType a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Return type"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix B says image_to_string returns a string (Sweigart, 2025). The pytesseract README documents an output_type argument with the values Output.BYTES, Output.DICT and Output.STRING (Pytesseract contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ReturnType> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReturnType" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TheFourLines .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReturnType .
pg:WhatComesBack a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "What comes back"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows the result for frankenstein.png and says Tesseract misreads two characters ('Iv' for IT and 'Motion' for motion), adding that the exact results may vary as the language packs are updated (Sweigart, 2025). It lists the issues to expect: end-of-line hyphens, no font information, odd white space, wrong characters and mixed columns (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_WhatComesBack> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhatComesBack" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ReadingAnImage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhatComesBack .
pg:FrankensteinOutput a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Frankenstein output"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints 14 lines beginning THE MODERN PROMETHEUS. 43 and CHAPTER V., and says Tesseract misreads two characters on the page (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_FrankensteinOutput> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FrankensteinOutput" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatComesBack .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FrankensteinOutput .
pg:LineBreaks a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Line breaks"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints the result with a blank line after the heading and after CHAPTER V., and says the whitespace in the string may not match the text (Sweigart, 2025). It also says the string does not conserve font or size information (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_LineBreaks> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LineBreaks" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatComesBack .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LineBreaks .
pg:ExactResultsVary a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Exact results vary"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter ends the listing with the remark that the exact results may vary as the language packs are updated (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract data files page says tessdata_fast holds integer models and tessdata_best the float models, and that the repositories differ in speed and accuracy (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ExactResultsVary> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExactResultsVary" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatComesBack .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExactResultsVary .
pg:LineEndHyphens a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Line end hyphens"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter lists the first issue to expect: the string maintains any end-of-line hyphenation, for example in dig- and ital or pro- and grams (Sweigart, 2025). It says always to consider OCR text imperfect and in need of correction (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_LineEndHyphens> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LineEndHyphens" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatComesBack .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LineEndHyphens .
pg:OtherOutputs a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Other outputs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter uses only image_to_string; the pytesseract README lists image_to_data, image_to_boxes, image_to_osd, image_to_alto_xml and image_to_pdf_or_hocr as well (Pytesseract contributors, 2026). The chapter mentions text embedded in a PDF only through NAPS2 (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_OtherOutputs> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OtherOutputs" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ReadingAnImage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OtherOutputs .
pg:ImageToData a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image to data"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not mention image_to_data. The pytesseract README documents it, with output_type=Output.DICT returning a dict of lists (Pytesseract contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ImageToData> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageToData" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OtherOutputs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageToData .
pg:ConfidenceScale a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Confidence scale"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not say how sure Tesseract is about a word, although it warns that OCR text always needs correction (Sweigart, 2025). The pytesseract README returns the conf column in the dict of image_to_data (Pytesseract contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ConfidenceScale> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ConfidenceScale" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OtherOutputs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ConfidenceScale .
pg:SearchableOutputs a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Searchable outputs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter makes a searchable PDF only through NAPS2, and says a common use of OCR is a PDF of scanned images with searchable text (Sweigart, 2025). The pytesseract README lists image_to_pdf_or_hocr and image_to_alto_xml (Pytesseract contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_SearchableOutputs> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SearchableOutputs" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OtherOutputs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SearchableOutputs .
pg:OsdOrientation a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Osd orientation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to rotate the image so that the lines of text are upright (Sweigart, 2025). The pytesseract README lists image_to_osd, which reports orientation and script (Pytesseract contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_OsdOrientation> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OsdOrientation" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OtherOutputs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OsdOrientation .
pg:AccuracyAndPreprocessing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Accuracy and preprocessing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The part Preprocessing an Image warns that OCR text is always imperfect and lists nine preprocessing steps: separate columns, typewritten text, conventional fonts, upright lines, dark text on a light background, no dark borders, a small white border, enough contrast and no noise pixels; it points to a blog post on OpenCV (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract documentation page on quality has sections on rescaling, binarisation, noise removal, dilation and erosion, rotation and deskewing, and borders (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_AccuracyAndPreprocessing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AccuracyAndPreprocessing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AccuracyAndPreprocessing .
pg:MeasuringAccuracy a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Measuring accuracy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter has no measure of accuracy; it says OCR output is imperfect and should be corrected and that mistakes in numbers are harder to spot than misspelled words (Sweigart, 2025). The character error rate is the Levenshtein edit distance divided by the length of the reference, and the word error rate is the same quotient over words (Wikipedia, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_MeasuringAccuracy> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MeasuringAccuracy" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:AccuracyAndPreprocessing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MeasuringAccuracy .
pg:EditDistance a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Edit distance"@en ;
    skos:definition "Wikipedia's article on the Levenshtein distance defines it as the minimum number of single-character edits (insertions, deletions or substitutions) that change one word into the other and uses kitten and sitting as its example (Wikipedia, 2026). The Python documentation of difflib offers SequenceMatcher for aligning two sequences (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_EditDistance> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EditDistance" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MeasuringAccuracy .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EditDistance .
pg:CharacterErrorRate a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Character error rate"@en ;
    skos:definition "A rate needs a reference. The study divides the edit distance by the number of characters of the reference after every run of white space has become one space, so that line breaks do not count as errors (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_CharacterErrorRate> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CharacterErrorRate" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MeasuringAccuracy .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CharacterErrorRate .
pg:WordErrorRate a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Word error rate"@en ;
    skos:definition "The word error rate counts words; the Wikipedia article gives it as the substitutions, deletions and insertions over the number of words in the reference (Wikipedia, 2026). The chapter counts mistakes only by eye, naming Iv and Motion (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_WordErrorRate> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WordErrorRate" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MeasuringAccuracy .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WordErrorRate .
pg:WhatHurts a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "What hurts"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says Tesseract is designed for typewritten dark text on light backgrounds where the text is level, that photographs and handwriting are out of reach, and that cursive and stylized fonts, tilt, noise and white text on black should be avoided (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract quality page lists scale, skew, noise and borders among the causes of poor results (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_WhatHurts> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhatHurts" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:AccuracyAndPreprocessing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhatHurts .
pg:SmallText a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Small text"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tesseract quality page says the engine works best on images of at least 300 dpi, so it may help to resize them (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026). The chapter does not mention size or resolution (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_SmallText> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SmallText" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatHurts .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SmallText .
pg:FontChoice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Font choice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to use conventional fonts, not cursive or stylized fonts (Sweigart, 2025). Kurzweil's company, founded in 1974, built the first omni-font OCR system, which Wikipedia describes as able to recognize text in any normal font (Wikipedia, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_FontChoice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FontChoice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatHurts .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FontChoice .
pg:Skew a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Skew"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to rotate the image so that the lines of text are perfectly upright and not skewed at a slight angle (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract quality page has a section on rotation and deskewing (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_Skew> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Skew" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatHurts .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Skew .
pg:NoiseLevel a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Noise level"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to remove small bits of noise pixels before scanning (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract quality page has a section on noise removal and says some noise cannot be removed by the binarisation step (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_NoiseLevel> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NoiseLevel" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatHurts .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NoiseLevel .
pg:LowContrast a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Low contrast"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to adjust the brightness and contrast of the image so that the text stands out from the background (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract quality page has a section on binarisation, the step that turns grey into black and white (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_LowContrast> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LowContrast" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatHurts .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LowContrast .
pg:WhiteOnBlack a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "White on black"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to use dark text on a light background, not white text on a black background (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract quality page says that for version 4.x one should use dark text on a light background (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_WhiteOnBlack> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhiteOnBlack" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatHurts .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhiteOnBlack .
pg:WhatHelps a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "What helps"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says some of the preprocessing steps can be performed automatically with Python using the OpenCV library, and points to the blog post Preprocessing Images for OCR with Python and OpenCV at autbor.com/preprocessingocr (Sweigart, 2025). PyPI lists opencv-python-headless 5.0.0.93 as the newest release, uploaded on 2026-07-02 (Python Package Index, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_WhatHelps> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhatHelps" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:AccuracyAndPreprocessing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhatHelps .
pg:GrayscaleConversion a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Grayscale conversion"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's contrast step can be done with Pillow's ImageOps, whose reference lists autocontrast, invert, grayscale and expand (Pillow Developers, 2026). The chapter itself does not mention grey conversion (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_GrayscaleConversion> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GrayscaleConversion" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatHelps .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GrayscaleConversion .
pg:Binarization a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Binarization"@en ;
    skos:definition "Wikipedia says Otsu's method, published by Nobuyuki Otsu in 1979, chooses a threshold automatically from the grey histogram (Wikipedia, 2026). The Tesseract quality page says version 5.0.0 added the Leptonica-based Adaptive Otsu and Sauvola binarisation methods (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_Binarization> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Binarization" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatHelps .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Binarization .
pg:Upscaling a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Upscaling"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tesseract quality page says the engine works best on images of at least 300 dpi, so it may be beneficial to resize them (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026). Pillow's Image.resize takes a size and a resampling filter (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_Upscaling> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Upscaling" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatHelps .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Upscaling .
pg:WhiteBorder a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "White border"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to add a small white border if the text runs up against the edge of the image (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract quality page has a section called missing borders, which says text without any border may give problems (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_WhiteBorder> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhiteBorder" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatHelps .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhiteBorder .
pg:Deskew a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Deskew"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to rotate the image so the lines are upright, and that OpenCV can do some of the steps automatically (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract quality page has a section on rotation and deskewing (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_Deskew> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Deskew" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WhatHelps .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Deskew .
pg:Segmentation a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Segmentation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says not to scan multicolumn images and to put each column into a separate image, because with tables or columns the string may mix the text and give it out of order (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract manual page lists the page segmentation modes behind --psm (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_Segmentation> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Segmentation" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:AccuracyAndPreprocessing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Segmentation .
pg:PsmModes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Psm modes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter never names a page segmentation mode; the Tesseract manual page lists them, from 0 (orientation and script detection only) to 13 (raw line), and says some are not implemented (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_PsmModes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PsmModes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Segmentation .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PsmModes .
pg:ColumnLayout a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Column layout"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's rule is not to scan multicolumn images; its Summary says Tesseract works with typewritten dark text on light backgrounds where the text is level (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ColumnLayout> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ColumnLayout" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Segmentation .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ColumnLayout .
pg:SingleLine a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Single line"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tesseract manual page lists --psm 7 as the mode that treats the image as a single text line and --psm 8 as a single word (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_SingleLine> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SingleLine" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Segmentation .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SingleLine .
pg:ConfigStrings a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Config strings"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter passes only the image and lang to image_to_string. The pytesseract README documents a config string that is handed to the tesseract command line, and a timeout argument (Pytesseract contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ConfigStrings> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ConfigStrings" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:AccuracyAndPreprocessing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ConfigStrings .
pg:WhitelistDigits a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Whitelist digits"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tesseract quality page names the parameter tessedit_char_whitelist as the way to limit the characters that are recognized (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026). The chapter says to pay special attention to mistakes in numbers (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_WhitelistDigits> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhitelistDigits" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConfigStrings .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhitelistDigits .
pg:UserDefinedDpi a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "User defined dpi"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tesseract quality page says the engine works best at 300 dpi (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026). The chapter does not mention resolution (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_UserDefinedDpi> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UserDefinedDpi" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConfigStrings .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UserDefinedDpi .
pg:TimeoutArgument a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Timeout argument"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pytesseract README documents a timeout argument that stops Tesseract after the given number of seconds (Pytesseract contributors, 2026). The chapter does not use it (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_TimeoutArgument> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TimeoutArgument" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConfigStrings .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TimeoutArgument .
pg:LanguagesAndData a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Languages and data"@en ;
    skos:definition "The part Recognizing Text in Non-English Languages says Tesseract assumes English by default, lists the packs with tess.get_languages(), explains that the strings are mostly three-character ISO 639-3 language codes, passes lang='jpn' to read frankenstein_jpn.png, shows the gibberish of the wrong language and joins languages with a plus sign (Sweigart, 2025). The installation section adds that installing all languages takes about 600MB (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_LanguagesAndData> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LanguagesAndData" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LanguagesAndData .
pg:LanguagePacks a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Language packs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints the result of tess.get_languages() as a list beginning afr, amh, ara, asm, aze and ending ton, tur, uig, ukr, urd, uzb, uzb_cyrl, vie, yid, yor, and says a pack is a file with the extension .traineddata (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract data files page describes the repositories that hold those files (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_LanguagePacks> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LanguagePacks" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LanguagesAndData .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LanguagePacks .
pg:GetLanguages a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Get languages"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix B answer 5 says tess.get_languages() returns a list of language pack strings (Sweigart, 2025). The pytesseract README documents get_languages(config='') (Pytesseract contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_GetLanguages> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GetLanguages" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LanguagePacks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GetLanguages .
pg:DataFolder a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Data folder"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to install the packs and to see which are installed with get_languages() (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract command line documentation documents --tessdata-dir for naming the folder that holds the data files (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_DataFolder> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DataFolder" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LanguagePacks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DataFolder .
pg:AllLanguagesSize a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "All languages size"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says installing all languages adds about 600MB to the install size, and prints a list that begins afr, amh, ara and ends vie, yid, yor (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract data files page says the tessdata_fast repository holds integer models for the languages and scripts (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_AllLanguagesSize> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AllLanguagesSize" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LanguagePacks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AllLanguagesSize .
pg:ChoosingLanguage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Choosing language"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter passes lang='jpn' for the Japanese page, shows lang='eng' giving gibberish on it, and joins codes with a plus sign, as in lang='eng+jpn' (Sweigart, 2025). Appendix B answers 1 and 6 repeat the default and the combination (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ChoosingLanguage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ChoosingLanguage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LanguagesAndData .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ChoosingLanguage .
pg:IsoCodes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Iso codes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the strings are mostly three-character ISO 639-3 codes with a few exceptions such as aze_cyrl, and the installation section says to use a three-character ISO 639 code such as fra, deu or jpn (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract manual page says the language is given as an ISO 639-2 code (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026); ISO 639-3 is the SIL table and ISO 639-2 the Library of Congress list (SIL International, 2026)(Library of Congress, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_IsoCodes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-IsoCodes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChoosingLanguage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:IsoCodes .
pg:JapaneseImage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Japanese image"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints the first two and the last two lines of the Japanese text read from frankenstein_jpn.png with lang='jpn', beginning 第 5 剖 and containing 芝鬱 (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract data files page lists jpn among the packs and the different data sets (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_JapaneseImage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-JapaneseImage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChoosingLanguage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:JapaneseImage .
pg:WrongLanguage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Wrong language"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that with the wrong language image_to_string returns Tesseract's best guess of what English characters the Japanese characters looked like, which is gibberish, and prints BS FABADOABOMEE as the start (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_WrongLanguage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WrongLanguage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChoosingLanguage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WrongLanguage .
pg:CombinedLanguages a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Combined languages"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to combine language codes with a + character, as in lang='eng+jpn', and Appendix B answer 6 says to pass lang='eng+jpn' (Sweigart, 2025). It says NAPS2's --ocrlang is passed directly to Tesseract, so 'eng+jpn+rus' works there too (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_CombinedLanguages> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CombinedLanguages" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChoosingLanguage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CombinedLanguages .
pg:MissingLanguage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Missing language"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not say what happens when a pack is not installed; it says only that the packs are installed with the instructions of its installation section (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_MissingLanguage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MissingLanguage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChoosingLanguage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MissingLanguage .
pg:AccentedLetters a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Accented letters"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's installation section names fra for French, deu for German and jpn for Japanese (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract data files page lists these and the other languages with their files (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_AccentedLetters> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AccentedLetters" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChoosingLanguage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AccentedLetters .
pg:ModelKinds a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Model kinds"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not distinguish kinds of language data. The Tesseract data files page distinguishes tessdata_fast, tessdata_best and the tessdata repository with the legacy and the LSTM engines, and says --oem chooses the engine (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ModelKinds> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModelKinds" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LanguagesAndData .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModelKinds .
pg:FastBestLegacy a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Fast best legacy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tesseract data files page says tessdata_fast holds integer models, tessdata_best the float models and tessdata both the legacy and the LSTM models (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026). The README of tessdata_fast calls it the fast integer version of the trained data and the README of tessdata_best the best trained models for LSTM (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_FastBestLegacy> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FastBestLegacy" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModelKinds .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FastBestLegacy .
pg:OemModes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Oem modes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Tesseract command line documentation documents --oem, with 0 for the legacy engine, 1 for the LSTM engine and 3 for the default (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026). The chapter does not use it (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_OemModes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OemModes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModelKinds .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OemModes .
pg:SearchablePdfs a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Searchable pdfs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The part The NAPS2 Scanner Application says a common use of OCR is a PDF of scanned images with searchable text, recommends the free NAPS2 application, and runs it from Python with subprocess.run() and a list of command line arguments, among them -i, -o, --install, --ocrlang, -n 0 and -f (Sweigart, 2025). It adds a mini language for the input and points to ocrmypdf (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_SearchablePdfs> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SearchablePdfs" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SearchablePdfs .
pg:Naps2 a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Naps2"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter describes NAPS2, Not Another PDF Scanner 2, as an open source application for flatbed scanners that can run Tesseract and add text to PDF documents without a scanner, and says it runs on Windows, macOS and Linux (Sweigart, 2025). The NAPS2 command line documentation describes the console mode (NAPS Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_Naps2> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Naps2" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SearchablePdfs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Naps2 .
pg:NapsPackage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Naps package"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to download the installer from naps2.com/download, and on Linux to install the Flatpak with flatpak install and run it with flatpak run com.naps2.Naps2 (Sweigart, 2025). The NAPS2 download page lists a Flatpak and the other installers (NAPS Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_NapsPackage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NapsPackage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Naps2 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NapsPackage .
pg:NapsCommand a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Naps command"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter runs NAPS2 with subprocess.run(naps2_path + ['-i', 'frankenstein.png', '-o', 'output.pdf', '--install', 'ocr-eng', '--ocrlang', 'eng', '-n', '0', '-f', '-v'], capture_output=True), and says the call creates output.pdf with one page whose text can be highlighted and copied (Sweigart, 2025). The NAPS2 command line documentation lists --ocrlang and --install (NAPS Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_NapsCommand> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NapsCommand" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Naps2 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NapsCommand .
pg:NapsOverwrite a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Naps overwrite"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says -f forces NAPS2 to overwrite output.pdf if a file with that name already exists (Sweigart, 2025). The NAPS2 command line documentation documents the console options (NAPS Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_NapsOverwrite> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NapsOverwrite" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Naps2 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NapsOverwrite .
pg:NapsInputLanguage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Naps input language"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter describes a semicolon-delimited mini language with Python index and slice notation: spam.pdf[0];spam.pdf[5];eggs.pdf and spam.pdf[0:2];eggs.pdf[-1] (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_NapsInputLanguage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NapsInputLanguage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Naps2 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NapsInputLanguage .
pg:OtherPdfRoutes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Other pdf routes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter ends the NAPS2 section by recommending the ocrmypdf package for creating PDFs with embedded text (Sweigart, 2025). The OCRmyPDF documentation says it adds an OCR text layer to scanned PDF files so that they become searchable (OCRmyPDF contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_OtherPdfRoutes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OtherPdfRoutes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SearchablePdfs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OtherPdfRoutes .
pg:PillowPdf a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pillow pdf"@en ;
    skos:definition "Pillow can save an image as a PDF; its file formats page documents the PDF writer and the dpi option (Pillow Developers, 2026). The chapter notes that NAPS2 adds the text, which a plain PDF of a picture does not have (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_PillowPdf> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PillowPdf" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OtherPdfRoutes .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PillowPdf .
pg:TesseractPdf a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tesseract pdf"@en ;
    skos:definition "The pytesseract README lists image_to_pdf_or_hocr, which returns a PDF whose invisible text layer holds the recognized words (Pytesseract contributors, 2026). NAPS2 does the same through hOCR (NAPS Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_TesseractPdf> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TesseractPdf" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OtherPdfRoutes .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TesseractPdf .
pg:OcrmypdfRoute a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Ocrmypdf route"@en ;
    skos:definition "The OCRmyPDF documentation describes the tool as adding a text layer to scanned PDFs (OCRmyPDF contributors, 2026). PyPI lists ocrmypdf 17.13.0, uploaded on 2026-09-28, which requires Python 3.11 or newer (Python Package Index, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_OcrmypdfRoute> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OcrmypdfRoute" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OtherPdfRoutes .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OcrmypdfRoute .
pg:SubprocessAndInput a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Subprocess and input"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says Python scripts can use the subprocess module to run NAPS2 with command line arguments, so that no application window appears, and that Chapter 19 covered subprocess (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation describes subprocess.run(), capture_output and the returncode attribute (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_SubprocessAndInput> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SubprocessAndInput" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SearchablePdfs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SubprocessAndInput .
pg:SubprocessRun a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Subprocess run"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls subprocess.run() with capture_output=True and notes that capture_output=False shows the status text (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says run() returns a CompletedProcess whose returncode, stdout and stderr are bytes unless text is set, and that check=True raises CalledProcessError (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_SubprocessRun> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SubprocessRun" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SubprocessAndInput .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SubprocessRun .
pg:ProgramNotFound a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Program not found"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter gives three paths for NAPS2: r'C:\\Program Files\\NAPS2\\NAPS2.Console.exe' on Windows, '/Applications/NAPS2.app/Contents/MacOS/NAPS2' with 'console' on macOS, and flatpak run com.naps2.Naps2 console on Linux (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ProgramNotFound> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ProgramNotFound" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SubprocessAndInput .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ProgramNotFound .
pg:BookAndPractice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Book and practice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The last part of the chapter adds the large language model step that fixes mistakes the engine makes, the Summary, seven practice questions and the practice program Browser Text Scraper, ocrscreen.py (Sweigart, 2025). Appendix B answers the seven questions: English is the default language, PyTesseract works with Pillow, image_to_string() takes an Image and returns a string, a photograph of a street sign will not do, tess.get_languages() lists the packs, lang='eng+jpn' joins two languages and NAPS2 makes PDFs with embedded OCR text (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_BookAndPractice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BookAndPractice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:LlmCorrection a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Llm correction"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says a spell checker will not find OCR errors, because it flags the accurately recognized characters of words misspelled in the original and misses errors that give correctly spelled words, and proposes a large language model with a sample prompt (Sweigart, 2025). It says to verify the model's output because it may miss mistakes or add new ones (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_LlmCorrection> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LlmCorrection" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LlmCorrection .
pg:RealWordErrors a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Real word errors"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names 'Iv' for 'IT' and 'Motion' for 'motion' as the incorrectly scanned characters, and explains that a spell checker would miss errors that give correctly spelled words (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation of difflib describes SequenceMatcher and its opcodes, which align two sequences (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_RealWordErrors> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RealWordErrors" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LlmCorrection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RealWordErrors .
pg:LowConfidenceWords a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Low confidence words"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that identifying character mistakes requires understanding context (Sweigart, 2025). The reCAPTCHA of 2009 handed the words that OCR could not read to people, one of a pair being a known control word (Wikipedia, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_LowConfidenceWords> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LowConfidenceWords" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LlmCorrection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LowConfidenceWords .
pg:ScreenScraper a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Screen scraper"@en ;
    skos:definition "The practice program asks for ocrscreen.py: a screenshot taken with pyautogui, cropped to the text portion and passed to PyTesseract, with the text appended to output.txt; the template has LEFT = 400, TOP = 200, RIGHT = 1000 and BOTTOM = 800 (Sweigart, 2025). The PyAutoGUI documentation says screenshot() needs Pillow and, on Linux, scrot or gnome-screenshot (Sweigart, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ScreenScraper> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ScreenScraper" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ScreenScraper .
pg:ScreenshotCrop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Screenshot crop"@en ;
    skos:definition "The template of the chapter takes pyautogui.screenshot() and crops it with img.crop((LEFT, TOP, RIGHT, BOTTOM)), the box of Pillow (Sweigart, 2025). Chapter 21's box tuple is left, top, right and bottom (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ScreenshotCrop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ScreenshotCrop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ScreenScraper .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ScreenshotCrop .
pg:AppendOutput a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Append output"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter asks the program to append the recognized text to the end of output.txt, calling open() in append mode (Sweigart, 2025). The template leaves that as a TODO (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_AppendOutput> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AppendOutput" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ScreenScraper .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AppendOutput .
pg:NoDisplay a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "No display"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says PyAutoGUI is covered in Chapter 23 and takes screenshots (Sweigart, 2025). The PyAutoGUI documentation lists the Linux requirements for screenshots (Sweigart, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_NoDisplay> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NoDisplay" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ScreenScraper .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NoDisplay .
pg:BookCorrections a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Book corrections"@en ;
    skos:definition "The book and the machines differ in several places a reader will meet: the white-on-black advice, the shipped helper script, the pins of Appendix A, the linked blog post and the topics the chapter leaves out (Sweigart, 2025). The study found each by running or reading, not by assumption (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_BookCorrections> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BookCorrections" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BookCorrections .
pg:InversionAdvice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Inversion advice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to use dark text on a light background, not white text on a black background (Sweigart, 2025). The Tesseract quality page says for version 4.x to use dark text on light background (Tesseract OCR contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_InversionAdvice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InversionAdvice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookCorrections .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InversionAdvice .
pg:ShippedScriptNames a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Shipped script names"@en ;
    skos:definition "The online materials contain preprocess_ocr.py, a script that imports pytesseract and cv2; the chapter does not name it but the topic is its Preprocessing section (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says a name that is never bound raises NameError (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_ShippedScriptNames> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShippedScriptNames" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookCorrections .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShippedScriptNames .
pg:AppendixAPins a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Appendix a pins"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix A pins pytesseract==0.3.10 and pillow==10.4.0, and says the pytesseract package is left out of the automateboringstuff3 package because it is fairly large (Sweigart, 2025). PyPI lists pytesseract 0.3.13 and Pillow 12.3.0 as the newest releases (Python Package Index, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_AppendixAPins> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AppendixAPins" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookCorrections .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AppendixAPins .
pg:PlaceholderLink a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Placeholder link"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter links to the blog post Preprocessing Images for OCR with Python and OpenCV at https://autbor.com/preprocessingocr, with no trailing slash (Sweigart, 2025). The page the link leads to is titled Image Preprocessing for Tesseract OCR (Sweigart, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_PlaceholderLink> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PlaceholderLink" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookCorrections .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PlaceholderLink .
pg:TopicsTheChapterOmits a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Topics the chapter omits"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not use the words psm, page segmentation, image_to_data, tessdata, dpi, threshold, deskew, whitelist, hocr or timeout, although it does use the words confidence and OpenCV (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_TopicsTheChapterOmits> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TopicsTheChapterOmits" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookCorrections .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TopicsTheChapterOmits .
pg:Interpreters a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Interpreters"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's code uses only calls that exist in every release of pytesseract and Pillow of the last years, and Appendix A pins pytesseract 0.3.10 and Pillow 10.4.0 (Sweigart, 2025). Pillow 12.3.0 requires Python 3.10 or newer, and its documentation lists the Python versions each release supports (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_Interpreters> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Interpreters" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Interpreters .
pg:PythonTargets a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Python targets"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Python 3.14 documentation lists what was removed, among it pkgutil.find_loader (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Pillow's Python support page lists the Python versions of each release (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_PythonTargets> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PythonTargets" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Interpreters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PythonTargets .
pg:PillowReleases a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pillow releases"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not resize or rotate images; the Pillow documentation of 12.0.0 lists Python 3.14 support among its changes (Pillow Developers, 2026). Preprocessing recipes written for older Pillow can be tested against the newest release with a few hasattr calls (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_PillowReleases> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PillowReleases" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Interpreters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PillowReleases .
pg:PytesseractReleases a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pytesseract releases"@en ;
    skos:definition "The PyPI page lists the releases of pytesseract; 0.3.13 of 2024-08-16 is the newest and the book pins 0.3.10 (Python Package Index, 2026). The pytesseract README documents the functions of the wrapper (Pytesseract contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch22#S_PytesseractReleases> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PytesseractReleases" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Interpreters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PytesseractReleases .
